.TH SSSD-SMARTCARD-SETUP 8 "2026-10-11" "sssd-smartcard 0.2.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sssd-smartcard-setup \- guided setup for SSSD Smart Card / PIV authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sssd-smartcard-setup
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sssd-smartcard-setup
configures the whole Smart Card integration on a domain member: packages,
domain membership, CA trust, the certmap rule in
.BR sssd.conf ,
Group Policy and PAM. It must be run as root.
.PP
The script is built around the ways setup scripts normally fail, and its
behaviour follows from that:
.TP
.B Identity resolution is a hard gate
If
.BI id\  user@domain
does not work, no certmap rule can help, so the script stops and says so
instead of writing a rule that maps to a user SSSD cannot see. Pass
.B \-\-test\-user
to make this check meaningful.
.TP
.B Every stage verifies itself
A stage that cannot prove it worked stops the run rather than letting the next
stage build on it.
.TP
.B Failure rolls back
Every file is backed up before it is touched. A failure restores all of them
and restarts SSSD on the previous configuration.
.TP
.B PAM is never edited implicitly
It is the one file that can lock you out of the machine, so it needs
.BR \-\-pam .
The command printed is the correct one for this distribution:
.B authselect
on the Red Hat family,
.B pam-auth-update
on Ubuntu 23.10 and later, and an exact
.I /etc/pam.d/common-auth
line on older releases, which the script will not write for you.
.TP
.B Idempotent and resumable
Re\-running is always safe.
.SH STAGES
.IP 1. 4
.B preflight
\(em root, distribution, the CLI, the log file.
.IP 2. 4
.B packages
\(em install the per\-distribution package groups and enable
.B pcscd
and
.BR sssd .
.IP 3. 4
.B join
\(em check or perform the domain join.
.B realm
prompts for the password; the script never reads or stores it.
.IP 4. 4
.B identity
\(em prove the domain, the trusts and the test user resolve. The hard gate.
.IP 5. 4
.B ca
\(em review and merge the CA chain into the trust bundle.
.IP 6. 4
.B card
\(em detect the card, then have SSSD's own
.B p11_child
read and verify it, which is the exact code path used at login.
.IP 7. 4
.B certmap
\(em derive a profile from the certificate, show the diff, apply it.
.IP 8. 4
.B gpo
\(em
.BR ad_gpo_access_control ,
and optionally
.B adsys
and certificate auto\-enrolment.
.IP 9. 4
.B pam
\(em print, or with
.B \-\-pam
apply, the distribution's command.
.PP
A verification pass follows:
.BR "sssctl config-check" ,
service state, an end\-to\-end mapping test, Kerberos readiness and a full
.B doctor
run.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Modes
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print every command without running any of them.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Diagnose only. Changes nothing.
.TP
.BR \-\-yes ", " \-y
Do not ask for confirmation.
.TP
.B \-\-non\-interactive
Never prompt; refuse anything that would need a prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Continue past failed gates, such as a broken trust, a missing CA or no card.
Use this knowing what you are overriding.
.SS Domain
.TP
.BI \-\-realm " REALM"
Kerberos realm, for example
.IR DOMAIN-B.LOCAL .
.TP
.B \-\-join
Join the realm if the host is not a member yet.
.TP
.BI \-\-join\-user " USER"
Account used for the join.
.TP
.BI \-\-sssd\-domain " DOMAIN"
The SSSD domain that owns the
.RI [ certmap/... ]
section. Defaults to the first entry of
.RI [ sssd ]\  domains .
.TP
.BI \-\-account\-domain " DOMAIN"
The domain the user accounts live in, when it differs \(em the trusted domain
in a one\-way trust.
.TP
.BI \-\-test\-user " USER"
User to prove identity resolution with. Strongly recommended.
.SS Certificates
.TP
.BI \-\-ca " FILE"
CA certificate to import into the trust bundle. Repeatable; pass the whole
chain.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca\-db " PATH"
Trust bundle path. Default
.IR /etc/sssd/pki/sssd_auth_ca_db.pem .
.TP
.BI \-\-cert\-file " FILE"
Work from an exported certificate instead of a card.
.TP
.BI \-\-module " PATH"
PKCS#11 module to use.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " FILE"
Use this profile instead of deriving one.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-name " NAME"
Name for the derived profile.
.SS Login mode
.TP
.B \-\-require\-smartcard
Smart Card only. Refuses without an interactive confirmation, because a wrong
card, reader, PIN or mapping rule then locks everybody out.
.SS Group Policy
.TP
.B \-\-gpo
Manage
.BR ad_gpo_access_control ,
setting it to
.B permissive
unless told otherwise.
.BR sssd\-ad (5)
documents the default as
.BR enforcing ,
so a freshly joined host already denies login to anyone the domain's GPOs do
not grant InteractiveLogonRight.
.TP
.B \-\-gpo\-enforcing
Set
.BR "ad_gpo_access_control = enforcing" .
Implies
.BR \-\-gpo .
Read the permissive\-mode logs first.
.TP
.B \-\-adsys
Install and refresh Canonical's Group Policy client. Ubuntu only. Joining AD
and dconf policy are available to everyone; other adsys features need an
Ubuntu Pro subscription, free for up to five machines.
.TP
.B \-\-cert\-autoenroll
Install
.B certmonger
and
.B cepces
for certificate auto\-enrolment from AD CS. Implies
.BR \-\-adsys .
.SS PAM
.TP
.B \-\-pam
Actually apply the PAM change. Without it the correct command is only printed.
.SS Skipping
.TP
.B \-\-no\-packages
Do not install anything.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ca
Do not touch the CA bundle.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-certmap
Do not touch
.BR sssd.conf .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Skip the verification stage.
.TP
.BI \-\-only " STAGES"
Comma separated list of stages to run.
.TP
.BI \-\-skip " STAGES"
Comma separated list of stages to skip.
.SS Other
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
.B sssd.conf
location.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " FILE"
Log file location.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what would happen, change nothing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sssd\-smartcard\-setup \-\-dry\-run \\
    \-\-test\-user jan.kowalski@domain\-a.local \\
    \-\-ca /root/pki/root\-ca.pem \-\-ca /root/pki/issuing\-ca.pem
.fi
.RE
.PP
One\-way trust: host in DOMAIN B, accounts in DOMAIN A:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sssd\-smartcard\-setup \-\-sssd\-domain domain\-b.local \\
    \-\-account\-domain domain\-a.local \\
    \-\-test\-user jan.kowalski@domain\-a.local \\
    \-\-ca /root/pki/chain.pem \-\-gpo
.fi
.RE
.PP
Full Ubuntu deployment including Group Policy and auto\-enrolment:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sssd\-smartcard\-setup \-\-realm DOMAIN\-B.LOCAL \\
    \-\-test\-user jan.kowalski@domain\-a.local \-\-ca /root/pki/chain.pem \\
    \-\-gpo \-\-adsys \-\-cert\-autoenroll \-\-pam \-\-yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
Diagnose an existing installation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sssd\-smartcard\-setup \-\-check \-\-test\-user jan.kowalski@domain\-a.local
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
if a stage failed or verification found problems. A stage failure rolls back
every file the run had touched.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/log/sssd-smartcard-setup-<timestamp>.log
Full log of the run, mode 0600.
.TP
.I /var/lib/sssd-smartcard/backups/<timestamp>/
Backups taken before any change.
.TP
.I /var/lib/sssd-smartcard/setup-state
Record of completed stages, used for resumability.
.TP
.I /var/lib/sssd-smartcard/generated-profile.yaml
The profile derived from the card, unless
.B \-\-profile
was given.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
bash 4.4 or newer, and root. On an unrecognised distribution, package
installation and PAM configuration are skipped and reported rather than
guessed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sssd\-smartcard (8),
.BR sssd.conf (5),
.BR sss\-certmap (5),
.BR sssd\-ad (5),
.BR realm (8),
.BR authselect (8),
.BR pam\-auth\-update (8),
.BR adsysctl (1)
